💡 **SUGGESTION: The 422 fallback retry is silent — no log line records that inputs were stripped**

`triggerWorkflowCompatible` strips the rejected inputs and retries transparently, which is exactly right for backward compatibility — but nothing is logged when it happens. On legacy callers (`ai-teammate.yml` stubs without `inputs.branch`) every dispatch now takes this catch path, and the tick log shows only the normal `✅ Triggered …` line. That makes gh-1540's branch-keyed concurrency contract silently diverge per repo, and the next "why did two runs queue concurrently on repo X" or "which consumers still run legacy stubs" investigation can't be answered from tick logs — the same observability gap this PR's own motivation (frozen ticks, `processed: 0`) was diagnosed through.

A one-line `console.warn` before the retry keeps the module pure (no deps threading needed) and follows existing precedent in low-level modules — e.g. `js/common/scm.js:1502` (`console.warn('SCM ADO: triggerWorkflow — could not resolve pipeline for: ' + workflowFile)`):

```javascript
} catch (e) {
    var fallback = stripUnexpectedDispatchInputs(inputs, e && e.message);
    if (!fallback) throw e;
    console.warn('SCM dispatch: 422 Unexpected inputs — retrying ' + plan.workflowFile + ' without: ' + Object.keys(fallback).filter(function (k) { return !Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(inputs, k) || inputs[k] !== fallback[k]; }).join(', '));
    return scm.triggerWorkflow(repoInfo.owner, repoInfo.repo, plan.workflowFile, JSON.stringify(fallback), plan.workflowRef);
}
```

(Or simpler: log the stripped key names captured by `stripUnexpectedDispatchInputs` if it returns them alongside.) Behavior is correct as shipped — this is purely about making the fallback visible.

